# Cross-language homophones: spellings that sound alike to speakers of
# different languages.
#
# One group per line, space separated. These are NOT the per-language
# homophones in languages/<code>/homophone.lst — those are words that rhyme
# within one language, and `hs` uses them. These span languages: "youtube" and
# "yutup" are the same sound written by an English and a Turkish speaker, and
# "boutique / boetiek / butik" is one word spelled by four.
#
# The technique is sound-squatting across languages, described in
# X-squatter (Valentim et al., ACM TOPS 2024, docs/papers/3663569.pdf), which
# found ~15% of such candidates carry TLS certificates against 7% for other
# squatting types. The `xhs` algorithm reads this file.
#
# Derived, not curated. Regenerate with:
#
#     python3 scripts/crosshomophones.py <wikipron-tsv-dir> datasets/phonetics/homophone.lst
#
# Source: WikiPron broad transcriptions (github.com/CUNY-CL/wikipron), 15
# languages, 1.2M pronunciations, grouped by IPA after stripping stress, length
# and syllable marks. Kept: groups of 2-6 typeable spellings drawn from two or
# more languages, minimum four characters. Data is CC BY-SA, from Wiktionary.
